{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788120683,"nonce":"yb7Ksh00o0rmAEtJsTmDvCW2+0c=","message":"Open-question resolutions (worker, in-session on BORGES):\n\n**Registry mechanism: attribute scan.** `[Verb(\"op\")]` on `static object M(VerbContext)`, discovered by scanning the mod assembly at startup (`VerbRegistry.RegisterAll`). Chosen over an explicit table so wave-2/3 specs add verb files without all merging through one registration site. A malformed or duplicate registration logs a warning and is skipped — a bad verb never takes the bridge down. Each verb declares threading via the attribute: main-thread by default (executes at the GameComponentUpdate safe point), `MainThread = false` opts into the poller thread and forswears Verse access.\n\n**Arg validation depth: typed getters, strict on type.** `VerbArgs` wraps the parsed `args` object with `Str/StrReq/Bool/Num/NumReq/Int/IntReq/StrList/Has`. Absent + fallback returns the fallback; absent + `*Req` is `bad-args`; present with the wrong type is `bad-args` **even for optionals** — the callers are programs, and silently coercing their mistakes hides them. Unknown keys are ignored (forward compat). Every getter failure names the key and expected type in `error.detail`.\n\n**State header: yes, on every response.** Every result carries `\"state\":{\"gameLoaded\",\"tick\",\"paused\"}` from the volatile snapshot (no Verse access off-thread). The agent learns when its command landed without a second round trip; costs a few bytes.\n\nDesign notes in the same spirit, recorded rather than silent:\n\n- `ping` is deliberately **main-thread** — the canary for the full loop every later verb rides (inbox -\u003e queue -\u003e safe point -\u003e outgoing -\u003e file). At the main menu it answers `no-active-game`, which is itself the correct signal; menu-time liveness is `status`'s job (off-thread, like `version`, so the bench is observable before a game loads).\n- `ping` takes an optional `echo` arg — gives the args plumbing and the bad-args path a live test surface.\n- Results and status.json are written atomically (tmp + rename), so a poller never reads a partial file. The template wrote results directly; the CLI (1.4) polls results hardest, so the race class is closed here.\n- `version` reports `{game, mod, bench, sid}` with bench = machine name. Oddity for the record: `VersionControl.CurrentVersionStringWithRev` says `1.6.4871 rev591` while the depot `Version.txt` says `rev590` — both genuine, the internal rev is a per-build counter (same class of field the BORGES/dorian handoff doc catalogues; do not use either as a cross-machine build identity).","files":null}]}